<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boyacá Department</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Boyacá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boyacá <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> is a department of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> in the Eastern Ranges of the Andes, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a>. Its high plateau was the northern heartland of the Muisca, whose towns included Hunza, Sugamuxi and Tundama, the modern Tunja, Sogamoso and Duitama. It gives its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca">Battle of Boyacá</a> of 1819, which secured the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boyaca-department.jpg" alt="Boyacá Department" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davemor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritacuba_.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Boyacá lies almost entirely within the mountains of the Eastern Cordillera, although its western end reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalena-river">Magdalena River</a> at Puerto Boyacá. It covers 23,189 square kilometres and borders Santander and Norte de Santander to the north, Arauca and Casanare to the east, Cundinamarca to the south and Antioquia to the west. The Cordillera rises to 5,380 metres in the Sierra Nevada del Cocuy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> also holds high plateaux, a small part of the Middle Magdalena valley and a part of the eastern Llanos. The Altiplano Cundiboyacense, shared with Cundinamarca, is densely populated. The name comes from the Chibcha word Bojacá, which means near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> or region of the royal mantle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Spanish arrival the territory belonged to the Muisca. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of the zaque of Hunza they lived mainly by agriculture and by mining gold and emeralds. The Spaniard Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada conquered the northern Muisca, whose last zaque was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquiminzaque">Aquiminzaque</a>, shared out the land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a> and made the indigenous people work for him. In 1539 the conquistador Gonzalo Suárez Rendón founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> on sites where the indigenous people had earlier had villages, and Tunja became one of the main political and economic centres of the Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>.</p><p>In the war of independence the region was a battleground between royalist armies and patriot armies led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>. The Battle of the Vargas Swamp was fought on 25 July 1819, and the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca">Battle of Boyacá</a> at the Puente de Boyacá on 7 August 1819; the patriots won both. Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> created a Boyacá Department in 1824. After the Granadine Confederation was formed in 1858 the territory became the Sovereign State of Boyacá, one of the original nine states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of Colombia. Civil wars, among them the war of 1860–1862 and the Thousand Days War, and changing constitutions, including that of 1886, then reshaped it until it took its present form as a department.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Boyacá is one of the thirty-two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> as its capital. It is divided into 13 provinces and two special districts, with 123 municipalities. Many rivers rise there, the most important being the Chicamocha and the Arauca, and it has several lakes, among them Lake Tota, Lake Sochagota and Lake Fúquene. El Cocuy and Pisba National Parks lie in the north-east. The department is known as the Land of Freedom because of the battles of 1819, and it has three universities, among them the Universidad Pedagógica y Tecnológica de Colombia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boyacá Department” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Muisca were Chibchan-speaking farmers of the high basins and valleys of the Eastern Ranges of the Colombian Andes, the Altiplano Cundiboyacense of present-day Cundinamarca and <b>Boyacá</b>, at 2,500 to 2,800 metres above sea level.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After a daring march over the Andes, Bolívar's army defeated the royalists at <b>Boyacá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-7">7 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Iraca, centred on Sugamuxi, the modern Sogamoso in <b>Boyacá</b>, was one of the territories of the Muisca Confederation.</blockquote><a href="/state/iraca">Iraca</a></li><li><blockquote>Tundama was a territory of the Muisca Confederation in the north of the high plateau, around modern Duitama in <b>Boyacá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tundama">Tundama</a></li><li><blockquote>Hunza, now Tunja in <b>Boyacá</b>, became the seat of the zaque, ruler of the northern Muisca territories, in the centuries before the Spanish conquest.</blockquote><a href="/state/zacazgo-of-hunza">Zacazgo of Hunza</a></li></ul></section>
